  {
   "type": "text",
   "title": "Review: Kubernetes + NVIDIA for GPU workloads",
   "options": {
    "mode": "markdown",
    "content": "\n**Goal.** Show that Kubernetes with NVIDIA's GPU Operator makes GPU work easier to run than the traditional manual way: same model (Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct, LoRA fine-tune, served with vLLM), done twice.\n\n**Machine.** One RTX 4070 (12 GB), native Ubuntu 24.04, driver 590.48.01, k3s, GPU Operator v26.7.2. All numbers below are **single runs on this machine**.\n\n**What we proved**\n- **Self-healing:** a killed training job stays dead on the manual path; on Kubernetes it is training again in about 28 s with nobody involved (force-deleted pod; 27 and 28 s in two runs). A killed server is back and answering in 77 to 81 s.\n- **Sharing:** one GPU, six models. GPU busy 38% for one model alone, 93% with six sharing the card (same traffic per model). All six answer in about 0.4 s.\n- **Visibility:** live GPU and pod metrics (this dashboard) come with the stack.\n- **No penalty:** speed and GPU utilization are the same as the manual way.\n\n**What we did not prove:** Kubernetes is not faster, and the first-time setup took about twice as long (images). Growing (more nodes) and Moving (another cloud) were not tested. MIG is covered in theory only.\n"
   },
   "id": 1,
   "gridPos": {
    "x": 0,
    "y": 0,
    "w": 24,
    "h": 9
   }
  },
  {
   "type": "table",
   "title": "The six metrics: traditional vs Kubernetes + NVIDIA",
   "datasource": {
    "type": "grafana-testdata-datasource",
    "uid": "testdata"
   },
   "targets": [
    {
     "refId": "A",
     "datasource": {
      "type": "grafana-testdata-datasource",
      "uid": "testdata"
     },
     "scenarioId": "csv_content",
     "csvContent": "Metric,Traditional,Kubernetes + NVIDIA,What we measured,Status\nSharing,One job blocks the GPU,Jobs share it,Default scheduler: second pod stays Pending. Time slicing: scheduled in 9 s; six tiny models ran on one card at the same time GPU busy 38% for one model alone vs 93% with six sharing the card (same traffic per model) and all six answered in about 0.4 s. MPS added per-pod memory limits.,Proven\nFailures,Someone gets a message,It restarts itself,Simulated crash (kill -9 by hand; pod force-deleted on Kubernetes with no grace period). Training: by hand it stays dead until a person reruns it (finishing took 325 s); on Kubernetes training is running again about 28 s after the kill (27 and 28 s in two runs) from its last checkpoint with nobody involved; a graceful delete took 46 to 51 s. Serving: a person restarting by hand answers in 28 s; Kubernetes answered in 77 to 81 s with nobody there. Tiny-model pod kill: 39 of 40 requests OK and 6 of 6 ready in 72 s. Recovery time is mostly model startup. Two full rehearsals (28 of 28 checks each).,Proven\nWatching,Check a terminal,Live dashboards,DCGM exporter Prometheus and Grafana came up in 53 s: live GPU busy memory power temperature pod status and restarts (this dashboard). Metrics every 1 s. Panels confirmed showing live data.,Proven\nMoving,Rewrite for each cloud,Same setup runs anywhere,Kubernetes is a standard API so the same manifests apply on any conformant cluster: on-premises or in any cloud. We moved the same stack between two different hosts (WSL2 and native Ubuntu) with 0 changed lines in 4 manifests. Not run on another cloud.,By design\nSetting up,By hand on every machine,Set once applied everywhere,The same stack was set up on two different hosts: WSL2 on Windows and native Ubuntu. Same Helm install and the same manifests (0 changed lines in 4 files). On native Ubuntu the Operator found the GPU in 33 s with no workarounds (WSL2 needed 3). It runs on top of the host driver and toolkit. scripts/bootstrap.sh repeats the setup on another Ubuntu box (checked as a re-run here).,Proven\nGrowing,Rebuild a machine,Add a machine,Nodes join a running cluster in about 65 to 86 s and Kubernetes places pods on them with no manual steps (tested with 3 CPU-only VMs). The GPU Operator gave those VMs only hardware discovery and none of the GPU components because they have no NVIDIA GPU. A GPU node joining would get the full set automatically by design. Not tested: we have one GPU so no GPU capacity was added.,By design"
    }
   ],
